messages:
maria: Heads-up — CI flagged the gpu-nodepool chart after my helper refactor. Deploys passed but pods kept landing on cpu nodes.

sam: ugh. repro steps?

maria: helm template with our values produced nodeSelector: {} in the rendered podSpec for the GPU daemonset, so scheduler ignored the preference. locally I had nodeSelector but in CI it got dropped.

nate: did the values get merged weirdly by the include helper? we've hit nil path issues before when using include + toYaml.

maria: yep that was my guess. helper looks like this:
```tpl
{{- define "common.nodeSelector" -}}
{{- if .Values.nodePool }}
{{- include "common.labels" . | fromYaml }}
{{- else }}
{}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
```
But when .Values.nodePool is an empty map CI linter (chart-verifier) complains and Helm renders an empty object which kube ignores.

irene: empty map vs null is always annoying. chart-testing + kube-linter will treat {} different from null. our values.schema.json also had "required" on nodePool which made the linter fail when it was omitted in some overlays.

linter-bot: chart-verifier: ERROR: values.schema.json: nodePool: missing required property when overlay omitted.

sam: two problems then: 1) templating returns {} and breaks scheduling; 2) schema requires property so overlays must include it.

nate: fix ideas: prefer to render nothing when nodeSelector is empty, or render a specific label only when present. don't render {}. For example:
```tpl
{{- define "common.nodeSelector" -}}
{{- $ns := .Values.nodePool | default nil }}
{{- if $ns }}
nodeSelector:
  {{- toYaml $ns | nindent 2 }}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
```
That way the field is absent unless we have something meaningful.

maria: ^ this looks good. I also think we should stop using include | fromYaml for values merging — it's brittle if include returns an empty string. Use .Values directly and the sprig default helper.

irene: for schema we can relax required and instead use a oneOf/anyOf so overlays don't need to set it explicitly. e.g. allow nodePool to be null or object. CI will then pass for overlays that omit it.

sam: can you paste a suggested schema snippet?

irene: sure — in values.schema.json: 
```json
"nodePool": {
  "anyOf": [
    { "type": "null" },
    { "type": "object", "additionalProperties": { "type": "string" } }
  ]
}
```
This makes the linter happy when overlays don't set nodePool.

maria: I'll open a small PR that:
- updates helper to not render empty nodeSelector (use default + if),
- updates values.schema.json to allow null/object per irene,
- adds a tiny unit test in chart-ci that renders with empty overlay and asserts no nodeSelector key.

ops-jenkins-bot: build triggered for branch: fix/helm-nodeSelector-empty-1781234567 (job #4521).

nate: also double-check any places where we call include "common.nodeSelector" and then do something like `| indent` — that can create whitespace-only output which Helm treats as text. prefer to have the helper emit the full `nodeSelector:` block or nothing.

maria: noted. I'll make the helper return the whole block so callers can `{{ include "common.nodeSelector" . }}` and not nest it.

sam: one more thought — if this was causing pods to land on wrong nodes, we should check our autoscaler and GPU pool capacity thresholds. could be we rendered a toleration but not the nodeSelector so pods scaled on cpu nodes and then GPU pods stayed pending. ops team please monitor.

maria: good call. @ops-jenkins-bot can you run the GPU pool capacity dashboard for the last 24h?

ops-jenkins-bot: dashboard run queued: gpu-pool-capacity-24h. link: https://grafana.internal/d/xyz/gpu-pool-capacity

irene: thanks — once PR merges I'll run a dedicated canary rollout to ensure scheduling behaves.

maria: PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/charts/pull/789 — small patch + tests. PTAL and thanks for the quick triage. :tada:

sam: LGTM after CI green. :+1:

nate: thanks — nice catch, maria.
